  "prompt_text": "The figure is a broad overview of how soft materials can be designed for bioinspired electronics, linking chemistry, material behavior, computation, and applications. At the top, different molecular structures are shown as tunable building blocks with contrasting properties such as volatile versus non-volatile, ion-selective, and electronic transport types like unipolar, antiambipolar, and ambipolar. The message is that chemical design controls device function.\n\nThe middle of the figure emphasizes “from materials to function,” showing key behaviors of soft matter in operation: ionic injection changing conductance, transparency and color switching, mechanical traits like stretchability and self-healing, and rich input–output responses resembling biological signaling. It then connects these materials to computation in matter itself, highlighting evolvability, structural adaptation, and complex dynamics useful for neuromorphic or brain-like processing.\n\nOn the right and lower sections, the figure shifts to device-level goals and enabling technologies. It points to metrics close to biology, such as millivolt-scale responsiveness and energy efficiency, and to large-area, distributed systems. Example applications include artificial systems for learning and sensing, sensorimotor learning, environmental intelligence in plant-like networks, and neuromorphic bioelectronics that bridge biological and artificial domains, including biohybrid prosthetic-type systems.",
